<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#siculo-arabic">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Siculo-Arabic</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sicilian Arabic</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Siculo-Arabic was the variety of Arabic spoken in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-muslim-conquests">Islamic conquest</a> in the 9th century. It remained in use among Sicily's Muslims, Christians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> under Norman rule but died out on the island in the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. In Malta it developed into Maltese, the only Semitic language written in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> alphabet, and it left many words in Sicilian.</p></div>
